WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.189
So continuing to do and not as he says he
will do.

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:05.609
Donald Trump's 2019 deficit is on track.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:11.179
[inaudible] the $1 trillion mark, despite
Trump having told us, you might remember time

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:16.640
and time and time again that he's going to
fix the deficit that Barack Obama ruined,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.140
that he will pay off the debt altogether if
given eight years in office and the time that

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.090
he thinks he will need to do it.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:30.460
Now, anyone who understands these issues even
remotely knew that it was never going to happen,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.279
and in fact, it's getting worse and worse.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:36.899
As I've said before, though, we really need
to be reframing our conversations about this

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:38.940
issue of debt and deficit altogether.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.070
So let's start with the facts and then we'll
get into the analysis and the sort of meaning

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.250
of all of this.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:50.409
The White House itself is now predicting that
the deficit is going to exceed $1 trillion

00:00:50.409 --> 00:00:51.409
this year.

00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:53.890
The deficit has never hit $1 trillion in a
year.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:59.140
Now, anytime we talk about these numbers,
this is not a partisan, this is not to make

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.489
this worse or not as bad as it is.

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:07.680
This is just understanding when we hear the
deficit has never reached x number of dollars

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:08.690
before.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:13.780
We need to understand number one, uh, inflation
adjusted numbers so that we're really comparing

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:14.780
apples to apples.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.370
It's also important to understand the size
of the deficit as a percentage of the economies

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.530
total size and not in a vacuum.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.750
Uh, just a few things if we really want to
do this seriously.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.930
Uh, now let's go back to what Donald Trump
said that he would do when Trump was a candidate.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:34.770
He said, number one, he would eliminate the
deficit altogether and he would get us a surplus.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:39.020
Partially he would do it by cutting taxes
for the rich and corporations, which he said

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.320
would generate so much growth that government,
uh, ret tax revenues would actually expand.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.740
And there would be all this winning and we
would have a surplus and we would start paying

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:48.740
off the debt.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.630
Uh, that obviously never happened.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:55.030
And it's important to understand, like if
you want to talk about this at all, I hate

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.750
that I have to do this, but you really need
to understand the difference between debt

00:01:58.750 --> 00:01:59.750
and deficit.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:00.750
Okay?

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.380
And I apologize to those who are sick of me
doing this.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.120
But when I don't do this, and even sometimes
when I do this, the comments that I get in

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.380
reaction to debt and deficit stories are so
confused about the difference between the

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.380
two terms.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.290
So these are not the same things.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:23.999
Deficit is the one year, uh, a surplus versus
deficit that the country may have.

00:02:23.999 --> 00:02:29.299
Debt is the accumulated deficits and surpluses
over time.

00:02:29.299 --> 00:02:31.030
So let me give you an example.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:37.360
If in year one of a country, you run a $50
deficit, at the end of that, the country has

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.359
accrued a debt of $50.

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:42.130
You exist for one year.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.950
You ran a $50 deficit, your debt is now 50
bucks.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:53.209
If in the next year you run a $25 surplus,
what happens at the end of that year is your

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:58.849
debt has gone down from $50 to $25 right?

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:00.709
Year one deficit is 50 bucks.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:03.180
A year to surplus is 25 bucks.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.489
At the end of year two, the accumulated debt
is $25.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:11.779
You went $50 negative after year one.

00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:15.090
And you paid off 25 of it with your surplus
in year two.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.090
Okay.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.549
I mean, I, I, I apologize.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:20.709
Some people write in and they say, David,
please stop talking to the audience like they're

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:21.709
little kids.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:23.530
That's not what I'm trying to do.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.200
Every time I do this, people are confused
about the two issues.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.840
So we were told that the tax cuts would pay
for themselves because they would generate

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:38.900
massive growth and they would move us from
having a deficit each year to having a surplus,

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.269
which would start chipping away at the national
debt eventually.

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:48.099
If Trump has eight years in office, completely
paying off the national debt, now none of

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:49.629
that stuff has happening.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:50.629
Okay.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:55.689
The tax cuts have added to the deficit increases
in defense spending, which by the way were

00:03:55.689 --> 00:03:59.920
favored not only by Republicans, but even
by many Democrats as well.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.239
Those are increasing the deficit and we are
not on track.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:06.400
We're not on track to even pay off any of
the debt.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.209
Nevermind pay it off completely in eight years.

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:10.709
So those are the facts.

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:15.540
Now, I do think every time we talk about this,
we should consider whether it needs to be

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.540
reframed.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:21.259
And what I mean by that is the debt and deficit
is a political football.

00:04:21.259 --> 00:04:22.259
Okay?

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:27.030
Republicans say the most important thing is
paying off the debt and balancing the budget.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:30.960
When there's a Democrat in the White House,
regardless of whether that Democrat has actually

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.930
slowed down deficit growth, which Barack Obama
did when Republicans are running, they run

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:45.190
on promises to do this really important thing,
uh, start paying off a debt, generate a surplus,

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.319
but then when they are in office, as we saw
under George W. Bush, as we're seeing in a

00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:54.479
cartoonishly exaggerated fashion under Donald
Trump, uh, they just explode the deficit.

00:04:54.479 --> 00:04:57.430
And, uh, they always claim that they're doing
it.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.460
They either deny that they're doing it even
though we have the numbers and that's what

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:02.470
we see.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:07.889
Or they will argue that there were extenuating
circumstances whereby it made sense to a deficits

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:08.889
spend.

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:12.340
Like for example, when we decided we were
going to heroically spread democracy to Iraq

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.770
and, or punish Iraq for their non-existent
involvement in nine 11.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:17.770
Right?

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.409
The reason we why it made sense to spend all
that money in Iraq changed once we figured

00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:25.919
out Iraq had nothing to do with nine 11 but
still we were given reasons why it made sense.

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:31.449
So what we have today in the end is an unsurprising
status quo.

00:05:31.449 --> 00:05:38.539
We were told before Trump was elected, what
he wants to do with tax policy is not going

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:42.889
to generate all of this winning and growth
and start paying off the debt with surpluses.

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:45.680
In fact, it's going to increase the deficit.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.770
That is exactly what happened.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:48.960
None of this is surprising.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.930
So that gets us to the question of if and
how Democrats should talk about this on the

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:58.120
campaign trail because on the one hand Trump
made promises and he broke the promises and

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.520
he's doing the opposite of what he promised
to do.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:05.379
It seems as though exposing that on the campaign
trail for the eventual democratic nominee

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:07.150
would be a worthwhile thing.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:11.629
But on the other hand, we know number one,
that it's just a political football.

00:06:11.629 --> 00:06:17.800
We know number two, that in reality, the narrative
over deficit, bad surplus, good cutting, spending

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:23.880
good or whatever else, it's not actually a
modern and nuanced understanding of debt and

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:24.880
deficits.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.009
What we should be asking in reality is when
does it make sense for governments to deficits

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:32.229
spend and when doesn't it make sense?

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:38.930
So do we want to deficit spend to align the
bank accounts of the already rich and corporations,

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.210
including just providing money for them to
buy back stock?

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.530
The answer is no.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:48.889
Do we want to deficits spend to improve society
for large numbers of people improve their

00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:53.259
ability to live good healthy lives, have access
to health care work jobs that they like without

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:56.280
being tied to mediocre health insurance programs.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.599
Okay, now we have something to talk about.

00:06:58.599 --> 00:07:05.860
So I think that it is important for Democrats
not to ignore the issue of debt and deficit,

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:10.280
but we can't allow the right to continue framing
the narrative because they, when they talk

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:14.910
about it one way, when it's a Democrat in
office, they take office, then they just say,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.800
oh no, the numbers aren't actually what they
are.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.139
You can't believe the number, the numbers
that we're actually getting from the Congressional

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.610
Budget Office, uh, and from the treasury.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:28.449
They win with the framing that we have, and
lastly, please, please, please, can we stop

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:33.889
falling for this stupid idea that tax cuts
for the rich are so good for the economy,

00:07:33.889 --> 00:07:37.620
that they reduced the deficit and actually
increased government revenue.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.139
We didn't even need another example.

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:44.070
We had plenty of examples before Donald Trump's
tax reform plan, but we can now add the Trump

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:49.860
tax reform plan to the list of tax cuts for
the rich and corporations that did not cause

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.349
so much growth that they paid for themselves.

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:59.620
My question to you is, do you think Republicans
even believe this idea that tax cuts for the

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:05.449
rich pay for themselves or do they know that
it's bs, but just keep repeating it because

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:07.310
these are the tax cuts that they want?

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:08.550
That's my question to you today.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:51.040
Let me know

00:08:51.040 --> 00:09:22.820
what

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.010
you think.

